# Architecture Decision Records

Every architectural decision (storage, tenancy, auth, APIs, limits, deployment topology, test/runtime contracts) is registered as an ADR **before or with** the change. Updating `docs/design.md` is not a substitute.

## Immutable snapshots

Each ADR is a **point-in-time** record of system state and rationale. Once written, **do not edit** it. A later choice is a new ADR that supersedes the old one. Fixing typos in an uncommitted draft is fine; changing the decision in a committed ADR is not.

```
docs/adr/0001-sqlite-durable-object-per-tenant.md   # frozen
docs/adr/0002-capability-tokens-not-user-login.md  # frozen
docs/adr/0003-supersede-0001-sharded-channels.md    # new snapshot; 0001 stays
```

```
# ❌ BAD — rewrite history
edit docs/adr/0001-...md to flip the decision
patch docs/design.md and skip an ADR

# ✅ GOOD — new document, old one untouched
docs/adr/0004-yyyy-mm-dd-replace-long-poll-with-ws.md
Status: Accepted
Supersedes: 0001
```

## File format

`docs/adr/NNNN-kebab-title.md`. Next number is max existing + 1. Include:

- **Date** (the moment this was true)
- **Status**: `Accepted` (frozen). Never mutate an Accepted ADR to `Superseded`; the successor names the predecessor.
- **Context**: constraints and alternatives at that time
- **Decision**: what was chosen
- **Consequences**: what this snapshot implies, including rejected options

Do not land architecture without the ADR. Read prior ADRs; do not contradict an Accepted ADR unless a successor records the new state.
